WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.334 --> 00:00:02.667
-Our first guest tonight
is an Emmy-, Tony-,

00:00:02.667 --> 00:00:04.734
and Grammy Award-winning actor,
singer, and director

00:00:04.734 --> 00:00:06.667
you know from his work
in the show "Pose"

00:00:06.667 --> 00:00:08.234
and "Kinky Boots" on Broadway.

00:00:08.234 --> 00:00:11.334
His movie "Anything's Possible"
is streaming now on Prime Video.

00:00:11.334 --> 00:00:12.534
Let's take a look.

00:00:12.534 --> 00:00:15.601
-I keep telling her
there is no place safer

00:00:15.601 --> 00:00:17.634
than right here at home with me.

00:00:17.634 --> 00:00:19.100
-Mom, law of averages.

00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:21.133
-Fine. Fair enough.
-Wait, what is that?

00:00:21.133 --> 00:00:23.367
-It's just this rule we have
where I'm not allowed to ask her

00:00:23.367 --> 00:00:26.033
anything that the average mom
won't ask the average girl.

00:00:26.033 --> 00:00:27.400
-Mm-hmm.
[ Cellphone chimes ]

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:28.667
-Now I really got to go.

00:00:28.667 --> 00:00:30.467
It's so nice to meet you,
"Kha-leed."

00:00:30.467 --> 00:00:33.267
-Yeah.
-It's "Kha-lid."

00:00:33.267 --> 00:00:34.934
-What is it?
-Kha-lid.

00:00:34.934 --> 00:00:36.868
-Kha-lid?
-Mm-hmm.

00:00:36.868 --> 00:00:38.334
-You're both failing,
but that's okay.

00:00:38.334 --> 00:00:39.634
-You know what?

00:00:39.634 --> 00:00:40.834
-Please welcome back to the show

00:00:40.834 --> 00:00:42.868
our friend Billy Porter,
everybody.

00:00:42.868 --> 00:00:44.801
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:44.801 --> 00:00:51.100
♪♪

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:57.367
♪♪

00:00:59.934 --> 00:01:03.567
-Hello!
-Resplendent, as always.

00:01:03.567 --> 00:01:05.634
Billy, I'm so happy
to have you here in person.

00:01:05.634 --> 00:01:07.968
And congratulations
on this film.

00:01:07.968 --> 00:01:09.234
Not only did you direct it,

00:01:09.234 --> 00:01:11.434
you also wrote music
for the soundtrack.

00:01:11.434 --> 00:01:14.367
-I did.
We did the soundtrack --

00:01:14.367 --> 00:01:17.801
Republic Records,
who I'm signed to now.

00:01:17.801 --> 00:01:19.334
Getting ready
to do my own music.

00:01:19.334 --> 00:01:20.434
So get ready for that.

00:01:20.434 --> 00:01:22.534
-Very exciting.
Very exciting.

00:01:22.534 --> 00:01:26.033
-I'm back. The bitch is back
in the music business.

00:01:26.033 --> 00:01:29.901
But, yes,
Justin Tranter and I --

00:01:29.901 --> 00:01:32.300
Justin Tranter's camp
and myself,

00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:35.234
we wrote five new songs
for the soundtrack.

00:01:35.234 --> 00:01:41.267
And we also curated artists
who are queer.

00:01:41.267 --> 00:01:46.000
I think I could say
probably like 90% to 95%

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:48.868
of the artists on the soundtrack
are queer.

00:01:48.868 --> 00:01:51.968
You got to go listen
to the soundtrack, too.

00:01:51.968 --> 00:01:54.000
-Did you -- Was it --

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:56.234
Did you write the songs
and curate the songs --

00:01:56.234 --> 00:01:57.400
was that something
you were doing

00:01:57.400 --> 00:01:58.801
while you were making the film,

00:01:58.801 --> 00:02:00.267
or did you wait till you had
the finished product before?

00:02:00.267 --> 00:02:02.033
-Yes, we were doing both.
-Okay, gotcha.

00:02:02.033 --> 00:02:03.767
-You know,
I was writing along the way.

00:02:03.767 --> 00:02:08.434
Being a music artist
and a musical theater artist,

00:02:08.434 --> 00:02:10.934
I've always told stories
through music.

00:02:10.934 --> 00:02:14.968
And that's a really,
really important part for me

00:02:14.968 --> 00:02:18.667
as I take on
this director's thing.

00:02:18.667 --> 00:02:23.300
You know, it's inside of the
music that I find the rhythm,

00:02:23.300 --> 00:02:25.868
that I find the music
of the piece.

00:02:25.868 --> 00:02:28.067
-I certainly knew your
connection to musical theater.

00:02:28.067 --> 00:02:30.501
And that's somewhere, you know,
where you've always shone.

00:02:30.501 --> 00:02:32.167
I didn't realize
how young you were

00:02:32.167 --> 00:02:33.801
when you knew this
about yourself.

00:02:33.801 --> 00:02:36.501
But you were 15 years old

00:02:36.501 --> 00:02:40.200
when you went to audition
for "Dreamgirls"?

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:44.734
-[ Laughs ] Okay, so I was bit
by the bug in sixth grade.

00:02:44.734 --> 00:02:45.767
-Okay.

00:02:45.767 --> 00:02:50.734
-And "Dreamgirls"
came out that year.

00:02:50.734 --> 00:02:53.601
-Uh-huh.
-And I was obsessed.

00:02:53.601 --> 00:02:56.634
And I was trying to get out.

00:02:56.634 --> 00:02:58.334
'Cause I needed to get out,
honey.

00:02:58.334 --> 00:03:00.434
-This was getting out --
-Out of my house,

00:03:00.434 --> 00:03:03.200
out of my neighborhood,
out of all of it, right?

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:05.300
So, I was getting
"Backstage" magazine.

00:03:05.300 --> 00:03:06.901
For those of you
who don't know what that is,

00:03:06.901 --> 00:03:10.200
that's the trade magazine

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:12.767
that lists all the auditions
and stuff.

00:03:12.767 --> 00:03:14.133
-Gotcha.
-And so I was getting

00:03:14.133 --> 00:03:17.601
that magazine at my house
at 15 in Pittsburgh.

00:03:17.601 --> 00:03:21.200
And I would circle the auditions
that I would go to

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:24.367
if I had lived in New York City
at the time

00:03:24.367 --> 00:03:25.767
and if I was old enough.

00:03:25.767 --> 00:03:27.767
So one day when I was 16,

00:03:27.767 --> 00:03:31.934
a "Dreamgirls" audition came up,
and it was on a Friday.

00:03:31.934 --> 00:03:35.634
And I had the Friday off,
and I had my own money.

00:03:35.634 --> 00:03:39.367
And I lied to my mother and said
I was going to a friend's house.

00:03:39.367 --> 00:03:42.501
And I went downtown,
bought myself a train ticket,

00:03:42.501 --> 00:03:47.801
rode the train for 13 hours,
went to the audition in Chicago.

00:03:47.801 --> 00:03:50.400
This is before cellphones
or anything like that.

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:52.934
They told me I was fabulous
and that I was too young

00:03:52.934 --> 00:03:55.267
and to keep my eye on the prize.

00:03:55.267 --> 00:03:56.901
And I went back on the train.

00:03:56.901 --> 00:03:58.767
I went right back
to the train station

00:03:58.767 --> 00:04:01.834
and came back home,
crying for 13 hours.

00:04:01.834 --> 00:04:03.267
[ Audience aws ]

00:04:03.267 --> 00:04:06.567
-And when you got home, did your
mom say, "That took a while"?

00:04:06.567 --> 00:04:12.334
-No, she didn't know until,
I would say, maybe right now.

00:04:12.334 --> 00:04:15.000
[ Laughter ]
No.

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:18.167
No, she --
[ Applause ]

00:04:18.167 --> 00:04:19.434
She has known.

00:04:19.434 --> 00:04:21.501
I told this story at some point
somewhere else,

00:04:21.501 --> 00:04:24.234
and she was like,
"What did you do?"

00:04:24.234 --> 00:04:28.200
Said, "It's over, darling.
I'm 30 years old now."

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:30.100
-But, so, it's interesting,
'cause you were talking about,

00:04:30.100 --> 00:04:32.167
you know, wanting
to get out of Pittsburgh,

00:04:32.167 --> 00:04:34.267
and then, though,
you ended up going to college

00:04:34.267 --> 00:04:37.367
at Carnegie Mellon, which is
a wonderful theater school.

00:04:37.367 --> 00:04:39.234
-One of the greatest
in the world.

00:04:39.234 --> 00:04:41.033
-How old were you
when you realized

00:04:41.033 --> 00:04:42.400
it was right in your backyard?

00:04:42.400 --> 00:04:44.067
-I was 17.
-Uh-huh.

00:04:44.067 --> 00:04:47.267
-And I was in drama school,

00:04:47.267 --> 00:04:49.467
the Creative and Performing Arts
high school,

00:04:49.467 --> 00:04:51.334
where we shot the movie,
actually.

00:04:51.334 --> 00:04:52.534
-Yes.

00:04:52.534 --> 00:04:54.701
-Not the same building,
but the same school.

00:04:54.701 --> 00:04:58.334
And my drama teacher and mentor

00:04:58.334 --> 00:05:02.300
found out that I was planning
on graduating from high school

00:05:02.300 --> 00:05:03.834
and just going to New York.

00:05:03.834 --> 00:05:07.834
And I got yanked by my ear,
and they were like,

00:05:07.834 --> 00:05:09.767
"You're not going anywhere
but Carnegie Mellon."

00:05:09.767 --> 00:05:12.200
And I was like, "What?"
You know.

00:05:12.200 --> 00:05:13.767
And it was
right down the street.

00:05:13.767 --> 00:05:15.834
And this is what I mean --
this is what we mean

00:05:15.834 --> 00:05:18.567
when we talk about opportunity
and access.

00:05:18.567 --> 00:05:22.667
I lived 12 -- a 12-minute drive
away from Carnegie Mellon

00:05:22.667 --> 00:05:25.033
and had no idea

00:05:25.033 --> 00:05:28.133
that it was one of the greatest
drama schools in the world.

00:05:28.133 --> 00:05:31.033
Right? It wasn't until
some white people told me.

00:05:31.033 --> 00:05:32.901
[ Laughter ]

00:05:32.901 --> 00:05:36.934
So I'm grateful.
You know, I'm thankful for that.

00:05:36.934 --> 00:05:39.968
You know, 'cause they said, you
know, "You sing like a dream,

00:05:39.968 --> 00:05:42.300
you know,
you dance your booty off,

00:05:42.300 --> 00:05:44.868
and every time you open up
your mouth to speak,

00:05:44.868 --> 00:05:47.367
it's a disaster."
[ Laughter ]

00:05:47.367 --> 00:05:49.200
"You need to go learn
how to act."

00:05:49.200 --> 00:05:51.934
I'm so grateful somebody,
you know, was looking out.

00:05:51.934 --> 00:05:54.300
I call them my angels.
They were looking out for me.

00:05:54.300 --> 00:05:57.167
-Well, I mean, that is
the best thing a teacher can do.

00:05:57.167 --> 00:05:59.434
Sure, they can teach anybody,
but the thing about talent,

00:05:59.434 --> 00:06:00.901
right, you either have it
or you don't.

00:06:00.901 --> 00:06:02.834
And I think sometimes teachers,
when they recognize it,

00:06:02.834 --> 00:06:05.167
they're like, "Look, we're going
to help you down the path,

00:06:05.167 --> 00:06:06.567
but you're going to realize
your own talent.

00:06:06.567 --> 00:06:07.701
We're not going to
give it to you,

00:06:07.701 --> 00:06:09.267
but we know the place
to point you."

00:06:09.267 --> 00:06:11.834
-I was so blessed
to have people like that.

00:06:11.834 --> 00:06:13.601
-I want to talk about the movie

00:06:13.601 --> 00:06:15.033
and how you went back
and did it in Pittsburgh.

00:06:15.033 --> 00:06:17.801
We'll be right back
with more from Billy Porter.

